# Design — retired requirements — {{ project_name }}

One line per requirement the **owner** has taken out of the design, with the
reason. Append only; nothing here is ever edited.

**Only the owner may land a change to this file.** Not by approval — by
AUTHORSHIP of the pull request, enforced by `.github/scripts/owner-authored.sh`,
the same check that guards [`DESIGN.md`](DESIGN.md) and
[`VISION.md`](VISION.md). An agent may write the line and push the branch. It
may not open the pull request.

## Why it works this way

Both design documents are append-only, so a requirement that turns out to be
wrong cannot be edited away: it stays on its page and goes on reading like a
live requirement of the design forever. `coverage.sh` counted it as one — it
reported the id unplanned on every run, and the delivery driver, which
commissions planning for exactly those gaps, dispatched a planner for the dead
requirement every cycle and could never walk past it. A full unattended session
per turn, observed live.

Deleting the decision was considered and rejected: plans cite requirement ids,
later decisions cite earlier ones, and the backlog and the escapes ledger cite
both — so removing one leaves every citation pointing at nothing. Recording the
retirement here removes the requirement from the design's shape while leaving
the whole record standing.

## Why only the owner

This file is the one place in the repository where a line REMOVES a requirement
from what the project has to satisfy.

That makes it the one operation that can turn a red pipeline green by dropping
the requirement instead of meeting it. Every other route to green costs work;
this one costs a sentence. So it sits behind the strongest lock the project
has — and behind the only party whose judgement the whole arrangement is built
to serve.

An agent may **suggest** a retirement. It writes suggestions to
[`oracle/retirement-suggestions.md`](oracle/retirement-suggestions.md), which no
script and no other agent reads. A suggestion changes nothing, gates nothing,
and stops nothing; the owner reads it when they are back and decides.

## Format

One line each, and the id in backticks exactly as the design spells it:

    - `R<n>` — retired — YYYY-MM-DD — why it is no longer required

Say why in the owner's own words. This file is the only place that reason is
written down, and a later reader asking "why doesn't this do X any more?" has
nowhere else to look.

